1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,739
An open-source LLM project evaluates every
prompt change against a model provider.

2
00:00:09,779 --> 00:00:14,052
The job needs an API key. For pull
requests from the team it works.

3
00:00:14,092 --> 00:00:19,459
For pull requests from outside contributors,
which are exactly the contributions the

4
00:00:19,499 --> 00:00:24,738
project most wants to evaluate, it fails.
Somebody finds a trigger under which the

5
00:00:24,778 --> 00:00:28,665
job does receive the secrets,
and proposes a two-line change.

6
00:00:29,005 --> 00:00:33,231
That change runs a stranger's code
with your API keys and your token.

7
00:00:33,271 --> 00:00:38,176
The course's research identifies the
pattern behind several compromises, some of

8
00:00:38,216 --> 00:00:42,936
them in ML projects. Today you learn to
describe the collision, to refuse the

9
00:00:42,976 --> 00:00:46,707
workaround with reasons, and to
offer designs you can defend.

10
00:00:46,747 --> 00:00:51,158
Hold on to one question for the whole
video: whose code runs, with whose

11
00:00:51,198 --> 00:00:54,002
credentials? It sorts
every design we look at.

12
00:00:55,842 --> 00:00:59,715
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:59,755 --> 00:01:04,783
Four words first. CI is the automation
that runs checks on every change, and on

14
00:01:04,823 --> 00:01:10,108
GitHub it is called GitHub Actions. A
workflow is the file that says which jobs run

15
00:01:10,148 --> 00:01:15,498
when an event happens. A fork is an outside
contributor's own copy of the repository

16
00:01:15,538 --> 00:01:20,887
on GitHub. And a secret is a value, such as
an API key, that GitHub stores and hands

17
00:01:20,927 --> 00:01:21,464
to a job.

18
00:01:21,804 --> 00:01:26,820
This is the last content video of
Chapter 28. It has no new Git commands.

19
00:01:26,860 --> 00:01:32,429
It takes what you learned in Part 6 and
Part 7, about fork pull requests in video

20
00:01:32,469 --> 00:01:38,038
157 and about secrets and self-hosted
runners in video 160, and applies it to the

21
00:01:38,078 --> 00:01:43,579
three things an ML repository needs from
CI that an ordinary service does not: a

22
00:01:43,619 --> 00:01:49,257
GPU, a multi-gigabyte model, and a paid
API key. Each of the three collides with a

23
00:01:49,297 --> 00:01:51,058
default of GitHub Actions.

24
00:01:51,398 --> 00:01:56,457
Then three shorter subjects: what a
model-serving repository records about the model

25
00:01:56,497 --> 00:02:01,556
it serves, the leak paths that are specific
to AI repositories, and AI coding agents

26
00:02:01,596 --> 00:02:02,527
as contributors.

27
00:02:02,867 --> 00:02:06,513
Layer label for most of this
video: GitHub Actions and GitHub.

28
00:02:06,553 --> 00:02:11,389
The facts about them come from the course's
research report, as the textbook gives

29
00:02:11,429 --> 00:02:13,589
them. Nothing here was run on GitHub.

30
00:02:15,429 --> 00:02:17,008
Here's what you'll walk away with.

31
00:02:17,348 --> 00:02:18,760
After this video you can:

32
00:02:19,100 --> 00:02:22,920
Design CI for a Python ML project
with a matrix and caches.

33
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:28,318
Explain why LLM evaluations on fork pull
requests collide with the fork security

34
00:02:28,358 --> 00:02:30,294
model and name the safe design.

35
00:02:30,634 --> 00:02:33,785
Make the case for and against
self-hosted GPU runners.

36
00:02:34,125 --> 00:02:37,880
Say what a model-serving repository
records about the model it serves.

37
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:43,049
State which controls still hold when an
AI coding agent opens pull requests.

38
00:02:44,889 --> 00:02:50,608
CI for ML, in one sentence. The CI of an
ML repository is ordinary until it needs a

39
00:02:50,648 --> 00:02:56,436
GPU, a multi-gigabyte model or a paid API
key, and each of those three collides with

40
00:02:56,476 --> 00:02:58,378
a default of GitHub Actions.

41
00:02:58,718 --> 00:03:04,736
The ordinary part. A Python version matrix,
installs driven by the lock file, and a

42
00:03:04,776 --> 00:03:10,283
dependency cache. A matrix runs the same
job once for each version you list.

43
00:03:10,323 --> 00:03:16,049
uv's guide pins its setup action to a
full commit SHA and installs with uv sync

44
00:03:16,089 --> 00:03:20,502
--locked. The course's workflow
4, Python tests, is that job.

45
00:03:20,542 --> 00:03:26,633
Video 147 went through it line by line. For
docqa, one more step runs sh ci/check.sh

46
00:03:26,673 --> 00:03:32,764
with the base of the pull request, and the
checkout needs fetch-depth: 0 so that the

47
00:03:32,804 --> 00:03:33,712
range exists.

48
00:03:34,052 --> 00:03:37,649
GPU capacity. A runner is the
machine that runs one job.

49
00:03:37,689 --> 00:03:42,780
GitHub-hosted GPU runners come in one
shape: a 4-vCPU machine with a single T4.

50
00:03:42,820 --> 00:03:47,780
Larger runners, GPU included, are available
only to organizations on Team and

51
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:53,105
Enterprise Cloud plans and are always billed
per minute, with no included minutes.

52
00:03:53,145 --> 00:03:57,392
That's enough for a smoke test of
a small model, not for training.

53
00:03:57,432 --> 00:04:02,197
Teams that need more run self-hosted
GPU runners, on machines they operate

54
00:04:02,237 --> 00:04:07,003
themselves, which is a security decision
before it is a capacity decision.

55
00:04:07,343 --> 00:04:11,627
Caches. A cache is a stored directory
that a later run can restore.

56
00:04:11,667 --> 00:04:16,856
Caches are free up to 10 gigabytes per
repository, and anyone who can open a pull

57
00:04:16,896 --> 00:04:21,438
request can read them, so a cache
isn't a place for anything sensitive.

58
00:04:21,478 --> 00:04:26,537
They suit package caches and small test
models, not multi-gigabyte checkpoints.

59
00:04:26,577 --> 00:04:31,895
Fetch large models by pinned reference from
the model store inside the job, or bake

60
00:04:31,935 --> 00:04:33,508
them into a runner image.

61
00:04:33,848 --> 00:04:38,745
LLM evaluations. An evaluation scores a
model's answers on a set of examples.

62
00:04:38,785 --> 00:04:42,913
An evaluation that calls a model
provider needs an API key in CI.

63
00:04:42,953 --> 00:04:47,594
The textbook names the maintained
tools: promptfoo's GitHub Action runs a

64
00:04:47,634 --> 00:04:51,633
before-and-after evaluation of
edited prompts on pull requests.

65
00:04:51,673 --> 00:04:56,698
DeepEval integrates with pytest and fails
the build when a metric falls below a

66
00:04:56,738 --> 00:04:57,340
threshold.

67
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:01,820
Inspect AI, Ragas and the
LangSmith SDK are also maintained.

68
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:07,533
CML, once the usual way to post model
metrics on pull requests, has had no release

69
00:05:07,573 --> 00:05:13,246
since October 2024. The report infers from
repository activity that it is dormant,

70
00:05:13,286 --> 00:05:15,615
and no official statement says so.

71
00:05:15,955 --> 00:05:20,974
Such evaluations differ from unit tests
in three ways: they're non-deterministic,

72
00:05:21,014 --> 00:05:23,910
they cost money per run,
and they need secrets.

73
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:29,023
The collision. If this is new to you, slow
down here, because it's the center of the

74
00:05:29,063 --> 00:05:29,366
video.

75
00:05:29,706 --> 00:05:34,031
Secrets aren't passed to workflows
triggered by pull_request from a fork.

76
00:05:34,071 --> 00:05:38,994
So the evaluation job fails on exactly the
contributions an open-source LLM project

77
00:05:39,034 --> 00:05:44,017
most wants to evaluate. The tempting repair
is to switch the trigger, the event that

78
00:05:44,057 --> 00:05:48,920
starts the workflow, to pull_request_target,
which runs with the base repository's

79
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:51,970
secrets, and then check out
the contributor's code.

80
00:05:52,310 --> 00:05:57,133
That combination executes untrusted
code with your API keys and your token.

81
00:05:57,173 --> 00:06:02,515
The report identifies it as the pattern
behind several compromises, some of them in

82
00:06:02,555 --> 00:06:06,730
ML projects: PyTorch's self-hosted
runners, Ultralytics, LiteLLM.

83
00:06:06,770 --> 00:06:08,481
Chapter 21A takes it apart.

84
00:06:08,821 --> 00:06:11,819
Defensible designs, from
least to most machinery.

85
00:06:12,159 --> 00:06:15,765
One: don't evaluate fork
pull requests automatically.

86
00:06:15,805 --> 00:06:21,062
Run cheap, secret-free checks on
pull_request: lint, unit tests, ci/check.sh,

87
00:06:21,102 --> 00:06:24,776
evaluation against a local
stub or recorded responses.

88
00:06:24,816 --> 00:06:29,248
A maintainer triggers the paid
evaluation after reading the diff.

89
00:06:29,588 --> 00:06:34,420
Two: evaluate after merge. Run the
evaluation on main and alert or revert on

90
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:35,125
regression.

91
00:06:35,465 --> 00:06:40,921
Three: separate data from code. If the change
is only to prompts or configuration, a

92
00:06:40,961 --> 00:06:46,090
trusted workflow can read those files as
data and run the base repository's own

93
00:06:46,130 --> 00:06:51,194
evaluation code on them. This is safe
only if nothing from the pull request is

94
00:06:51,234 --> 00:06:56,429
executed, which includes test files,
conftest.py, package scripts and anything a

95
00:06:56,469 --> 00:06:59,308
prompt template can make
the harness import.

96
00:06:59,648 --> 00:07:04,883
Self-hosted GPU runners. They concentrate
risk because they're expensive, long-lived

97
00:07:04,923 --> 00:07:09,908
and hold cached models and cloud credentials.
The configuration the report calls

98
00:07:09,948 --> 00:07:14,555
defensible: private repositories or
approval for all outside contributors.

99
00:07:14,595 --> 00:07:19,768
Ephemeral runners that perform one job.
Runner groups scoped to named repositories.

100
00:07:20,108 --> 00:07:25,302
And OIDC, where a job asks for a short-lived
credential, instead of long-lived keys

101
00:07:25,342 --> 00:07:30,472
on the host. GitHub's own guidance is that
self-hosted runners should almost never

102
00:07:30,512 --> 00:07:34,067
be used for public repositories.
That's the case against.

103
00:07:34,107 --> 00:07:38,859
The case for is the first pressure point:
a single T4 doesn't train a model.

104
00:07:39,199 --> 00:07:44,111
And one production rule for the key itself:
give the evaluation job its own provider

105
00:07:44,151 --> 00:07:49,005
key with a spend cap, separate from
production's. When that key leaks through a log

106
00:07:49,045 --> 00:07:53,485
or a compromised action, the damage is
a bill with a ceiling, not production

107
00:07:53,525 --> 00:07:53,957
traffic.

108
00:07:54,297 --> 00:07:58,584
Model-serving repositories. The
report found no authoritative layout.

109
00:07:58,624 --> 00:08:01,030
It found three sourced building blocks.

110
00:08:01,370 --> 00:08:06,090
Secrets never enter the image through
build arguments or environment variables.

111
00:08:06,130 --> 00:08:10,910
Docker's documentation says both persist
in the final image, and provides secret

112
00:08:10,950 --> 00:08:12,296
mounts for the purpose.

113
00:08:12,636 --> 00:08:15,820
Weights are fetched by pinned
reference, never committed.

114
00:08:15,860 --> 00:08:20,516
At build or start time the image downloads
the revision or checksum recorded in the

115
00:08:20,556 --> 00:08:21,138
repository.

116
00:08:21,478 --> 00:08:27,102
The base image is pinned by digest, and
.dockerignore keeps .git, .env, data and run

117
00:08:27,142 --> 00:08:32,765
outputs out of the build context. It's the
Docker analogue of .gitignore and matters

118
00:08:32,805 --> 00:08:38,362
for the same reason: a COPY . . with a
credential file in the context bakes it into

119
00:08:38,402 --> 00:08:38,901
a layer.

120
00:08:39,241 --> 00:08:44,129
The link back to Git is the image label.
Record the commit in the image and tag

121
00:08:44,169 --> 00:08:49,307
images by commit ID or release tag, so that
"which code is serving" has one answer.

122
00:08:49,347 --> 00:08:54,485
One caveat: the textbook names the OCI
annotation org.opencontainers.image.revision

123
00:08:54,525 --> 00:08:59,726
for this and marks the name as unverified.
It's given from the author's knowledge of

124
00:08:59,766 --> 00:09:03,718
the specification and isn't in
the research report or its notes.

125
00:09:03,758 --> 00:09:07,212
Confirm it in the specification
before you depend on it.

126
00:09:07,552 --> 00:09:11,633
The Java and backend side. An ML
platform is rarely Python alone.

127
00:09:11,673 --> 00:09:16,960
Three facts. GitHub's ignore templates treat
the two build wrappers differently: the

128
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,842
Maven wrapper JAR is ignored, while the
Gradle template explicitly un-ignores

129
00:09:21,882 --> 00:09:27,042
gradle-wrapper.jar, which Gradle's
documentation says is expected to be committed.

130
00:09:27,382 --> 00:09:32,440
The committed JAR is a review blind spot:
the one place in a typical Java repository

131
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:37,234
where a pull request can change executable
code that a reviewer can't read in a

132
00:09:37,274 --> 00:09:42,088
diff, which is why wrapper validation
exists. And Dependabot needs one entry per

133
00:09:42,128 --> 00:09:42,695
ecosystem.

134
00:09:43,035 --> 00:09:48,366
uv has had version updates since the
thirteenth of March 2025 and security updates

135
00:09:48,406 --> 00:09:53,540
since the sixteenth of December 2025, and
docker and pre-commit receive version

136
00:09:53,580 --> 00:09:58,975
updates only. One more unverified note from
the textbook: Maven's own documentation

137
00:09:59,015 --> 00:10:03,887
on distributing the wrapper wasn't
fetched. The statement rests on GitHub's

138
00:10:03,927 --> 00:10:04,477
template.

139
00:10:04,817 --> 00:10:10,180
Secrets in AI repositories. AI repositories
leak the same way as others, and through

140
00:10:10,220 --> 00:10:15,070
three extra paths: notebook outputs,
agent and tool configuration files, and

141
00:10:15,110 --> 00:10:16,099
evaluation logs.

142
00:10:16,439 --> 00:10:23,163
The scale, as the report gives it:
GitGuardian counted 28,649,024 new secrets on

143
00:10:23,203 --> 00:10:30,095
public GitHub in 2025, of which 1,275,105
were tied to AI services, up 81 percent.

144
00:10:30,135 --> 00:10:35,168
It counted 24,008 unique secrets
in MCP configuration files.

145
00:10:35,208 --> 00:10:42,185
And commits co-authored by Claude Code
leaked secrets at roughly twice the baseline

146
00:10:42,225 --> 00:10:47,681
rate. These are one vendor's
measurements of public repositories.

147
00:10:48,021 --> 00:10:53,313
The rule that doesn't change: revoke or
rotate first, then find out what the key

148
00:10:53,353 --> 00:10:58,445
could reach, then clean up. git rm
--cached .env followed by a commit isn't a

149
00:10:58,485 --> 00:10:59,711
response to a leak.

150
00:11:00,051 --> 00:11:04,831
AI coding agents. An agent that writes
commits is a contributor with a different Git

151
00:11:04,871 --> 00:11:09,651
client, unusual speed and no memory of your
conventions, so the controls that matter

152
00:11:09,691 --> 00:11:12,520
are the ones that don't
depend on the contributor.

153
00:11:12,860 --> 00:11:17,992
What the report establishes: by October
2026 agents are first-class actors on

154
00:11:18,032 --> 00:11:23,297
GitHub. Copilot can review pull requests
and, in preview, approve them, and the

155
00:11:23,337 --> 00:11:27,461
Copilot cloud agent opens pull
requests and signs its commits.

156
00:11:27,501 --> 00:11:32,767
DORA's 2025 research describes AI as an
amplifier of an organization's existing

157
00:11:32,807 --> 00:11:38,207
strengths and weaknesses, and recommends
small, frequent commits for AI-generated

158
00:11:38,247 --> 00:11:38,543
code.

159
00:11:38,883 --> 00:11:42,472
The textbook presents this table
as the report's inference.

160
00:11:42,512 --> 00:11:46,901
Two incidents from the report show
that the third and fourth rows aren't

161
00:11:46,941 --> 00:11:51,884
hypothetical. An automated account ran a
campaign in February and March 2026 that

162
00:11:51,924 --> 00:11:56,867
combined pull_request_target abuse, injection
through branch names and filenames,

163
00:11:56,907 --> 00:11:59,573
and prompt injection
against an AI reviewer.

164
00:11:59,913 --> 00:12:05,119
And researchers reported in April 2026 that
AI coding agents run as GitHub Actions

165
00:12:05,159 --> 00:12:10,109
could be steered by text in pull-request
titles, issue bodies or comments into

166
00:12:10,149 --> 00:12:15,099
revealing CI secrets. Unverified: the
April 2026 research was verified for the

167
00:12:15,139 --> 00:12:17,786
report only through one secondary article.

168
00:12:18,126 --> 00:12:22,758
Attribution: decide how agent-written
commits are marked, with a Co-authored-by

169
00:12:22,798 --> 00:12:27,606
trailer, a dedicated bot account, or a
signature. Then "which changes did an agent

170
00:12:27,646 --> 00:12:29,144
write" is a git log query.

171
00:12:29,484 --> 00:12:33,931
The practical conclusion, in the textbook's
words: nothing in this chapter becomes

172
00:12:33,971 --> 00:12:38,090
less important when an agent writes the
commits, and most of it becomes more

173
00:12:38,130 --> 00:12:42,577
important, because the volume goes up and
the author can't be asked what it meant.

174
00:12:44,417 --> 00:12:48,883
Hold one question in front of every design
in this video: whose code runs, with

175
00:12:48,923 --> 00:12:49,910
whose credentials?

176
00:12:50,250 --> 00:12:55,302
Quick quiz, before I answer it myself. A
pull_request_target job checks out the

177
00:12:55,342 --> 00:12:58,718
fork's code. Whose code runs,
with whose credentials?

178
00:12:58,758 --> 00:13:03,617
A, the team's code with the team's
credentials. B, a stranger's code with no

179
00:13:03,657 --> 00:13:07,614
credentials. C, a stranger's code
with the team's credentials.

180
00:13:07,654 --> 00:13:08,387
Your answer?

181
00:13:13,227 --> 00:13:15,914
C. Now the whole list, one case at a time.

182
00:13:16,254 --> 00:13:21,119
An ordinary CI job on a team pull
request: the team's code, with the team's

183
00:13:21,159 --> 00:13:26,155
credentials. Fine. A pull_request job
from a fork: a stranger's code, with no

184
00:13:26,195 --> 00:13:29,490
credentials. Safe, and
unable to call the provider.

185
00:13:29,530 --> 00:13:34,657
A pull_request_target job that checks out
the fork: a stranger's code, with the

186
00:13:34,697 --> 00:13:37,339
team's credentials. That's the collision.

187
00:13:37,679 --> 00:13:42,628
A self-hosted GPU runner on a public
repository: a stranger's code, on a machine

188
00:13:42,668 --> 00:13:46,556
that keeps state and holds cached
models and cloud credentials.

189
00:13:46,596 --> 00:13:51,608
An agent triggered by an issue comment:
instructions from a stranger, executed by

190
00:13:51,648 --> 00:13:53,416
something that holds a token.

191
00:13:53,756 --> 00:13:56,863
Each defensible design changes
one of the two halves.

192
00:13:56,903 --> 00:14:01,731
Either the stranger's code doesn't run in
the privileged job, or the job that runs

193
00:14:01,771 --> 00:14:03,512
it holds nothing worth taking.

194
00:14:03,852 --> 00:14:07,453
Where this model is too coarse:
"code" has to be read widely.

195
00:14:07,493 --> 00:14:12,406
A test file, a conftest.py, a package script
and a prompt template that the harness

196
00:14:12,446 --> 00:14:16,584
imports are all code for this purpose,
and for an agent, text is code.

197
00:14:16,924 --> 00:14:21,980
Try it now, on paper, for thirty seconds.
Think of one automated job you know, at

198
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:26,762
work or in a project you follow. Write
one line: whose code runs, with whose

199
00:14:26,802 --> 00:14:28,209
credentials? I'll wait.

200
00:14:33,049 --> 00:14:37,542
If your line says "anyone's code" and "our
credentials" together, you have found

201
00:14:37,582 --> 00:14:42,189
something worth raising with your team. If
you couldn't tell, that's worth knowing

202
00:14:42,229 --> 00:14:42,416
too.

203
00:14:44,256 --> 00:14:49,125
Start with the left box, and stop at its
last line: the evaluation can't call the

204
00:14:49,165 --> 00:14:54,216
provider. That's the contributor's complaint.
Then the right box, and its last line,

205
00:14:54,256 --> 00:14:58,944
slowly. The evaluation works. So does a
changed test file that prints the key.

206
00:14:58,984 --> 00:15:03,792
That's the two-line change from the opening,
and our question has answered it: a

207
00:15:03,832 --> 00:15:06,156
stranger's code, with your credentials.

208
00:15:07,996 --> 00:15:13,191
Into the lab, for four snippets. Nothing
here touches GitHub: it's the local

209
00:15:13,231 --> 00:15:14,844
repository of video 177.

210
00:15:15,184 --> 00:15:19,469
Step 1: the checks, the hook and
the CI entry point arrive together.

211
00:15:19,809 --> 00:15:25,704
One script, a hook that calls it, and
ci/check.sh. The checks: line at the commit is

212
00:15:25,744 --> 00:15:31,499
the hook. In a workflow, the same script
runs as a step, with the base of the pull

213
00:15:31,539 --> 00:15:37,364
request as its argument. This is the
secret-free part of CI: it needs no key, so it

214
00:15:37,404 --> 00:15:40,402
runs on every pull
request, forks included.

215
00:15:40,742 --> 00:15:43,331
Step 2: the evaluation writes a record.

216
00:15:43,671 --> 00:15:48,463
The evaluation here is local and
deterministic. An LLM evaluation is neither.

217
00:15:48,503 --> 00:15:53,546
Predict: which of the three properties,
non-deterministic, paid, needing secrets,

218
00:15:53,586 --> 00:15:56,621
would change this step,
and how? Say it out loud.

219
00:16:01,461 --> 00:16:06,208
The answer is the table of the concept
section. Compare against a threshold with a

220
00:16:06,248 --> 00:16:09,827
tolerance, not for equality.
Trigger on the paths that matter.

221
00:16:09,867 --> 00:16:11,637
And decide where the key lives.

222
00:16:11,977 --> 00:16:15,206
Step 3: the serving definition
and the model pointer.

223
00:16:15,546 --> 00:16:19,406
The weights aren't committed. The
pointer is: a checksum and a size.

224
00:16:19,446 --> 00:16:24,053
The Dockerfile and the .dockerignore arrive
in the same commit, and the commit is

225
00:16:24,093 --> 00:16:28,814
tagged. A serving repository pins the model
by an immutable identifier, and the tag

226
00:16:28,854 --> 00:16:29,674
names the code.

227
00:16:30,014 --> 00:16:31,433
Step 4: the result.

228
00:16:31,773 --> 00:16:37,726
Seven commits, three ignored paths, and
ci/check.sh over the whole history from the

229
00:16:37,766 --> 00:16:43,792
root commit: no problems, tests ok. That
last command is what a required check would

230
00:16:43,832 --> 00:16:44,081
run.

231
00:16:44,421 --> 00:16:48,498
Read its header first. It says four
things. It's teaching material.

232
00:16:48,538 --> 00:16:53,292
Don't use this workflow in a real
repository. It contains deliberately planted

233
00:16:53,332 --> 00:16:57,901
security weaknesses. And it was
parse-checked and never executed on GitHub.

234
00:16:57,941 --> 00:17:03,002
It describes itself: in a public repository,
it runs the model evaluation for every

235
00:17:03,042 --> 00:17:07,857
pull request on the team's own GPU machine,
which is registered as a persistent

236
00:17:07,897 --> 00:17:09,025
self-hosted runner.

237
00:17:09,365 --> 00:17:13,846
Then read it top to bottom: the trigger,
the permissions block, the runs-on line,

238
00:17:13,886 --> 00:17:18,368
the checkout with its pinned commit, a
step named "Debug", and the step that runs

239
00:17:18,408 --> 00:17:21,773
the evaluation. Stop the video
here and read it once, slowly.

240
00:17:24,113 --> 00:17:28,922
The weaknesses are the subject of an
exercise, and this video doesn't name them.

241
00:17:28,962 --> 00:17:33,892
Take the review checklist of section 21A.19,
the one from the Gate 8 briefing, and

242
00:17:33,932 --> 00:17:37,468
go down its eleven headings
with this file in front of you.

243
00:17:37,508 --> 00:17:42,438
For each heading, write "fine", "not
applicable" or a finding with one sentence of

244
00:17:42,478 --> 00:17:47,045
blast radius. Use the question of this
video: whose code runs here, on which

245
00:17:47,085 --> 00:17:52,075
machine, with what in reach? Then compare
